  "image_name": "2d01bcc055d85a61eda77df7b8489260.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes immune environments at the borders of the brain and links them to how cellular cancer therapy might behave in the central nervous system. On the left, a cross-section of the head highlights several border niches: skull bone marrow above the meninges, meningeal lymphatic vessels draining toward cervical lymph nodes, dural venous sinuses, the choroid plexus, and vascular or perivascular spaces within the brain. These regions are shown as connected routes and interfaces where blood vessels, lymphatic channels, and immune cells can move or interact around the CNS rather than deep inside the brain tissue alone. The choroid plexus inset especially emphasizes a cellular barrier rich in different immune and stromal components around a vascular core.\n\nOn the right, the diagram shifts to therapy, placing an adoptive cell therapy cell in the center and surrounding it with CNS-resident or tumor-associated cell types. Arrows indicate two-way interactions with microglia, neurons, oligodendrocyte precursor cells, astrocytes, and cancer cells, while myeloid cells and vasculature are also depicted as relevant influences. Overall, the message is that brain-border immune niches and local CNS cells together shape the activity of cancer cell therapies in the CNS.",
